DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       These  functions are provided in glibc as a compatibility interface for
       programs that make use of the historical System V signal API.  This API
       is obsolete: new applications should use the POSIX signal  API  (ssiiggaacc‐‐
       ttiioonn(2), ssiiggpprrooccmmaasskk(2), etc.)

       The  ssiiggsseett() function modifies the disposition of the signal _s_i_g.  The
       _d_i_s_p argument can be the address of a signal handler function,  or  one
       of the following constants:

       SSIIGG__DDFFLL
              Reset the disposition of _s_i_g to the default.

       SSIIGG__IIGGNN
              Ignore _s_i_g.

       SSIIGG__HHOOLLDD
              Add  _s_i_g to the process’s signal mask, but leave the disposition
              of _s_i_g unchanged.

       If _d_i_s_p specifies the address of a signal handler, then _s_i_g is added to
       the process’s signal mask during execution of the handler.

       If _d_i_s_p was specified as a value other than SSIIGG__HHOOLLDD, then _s_i_g  is  re‐
       moved from the process’s signal mask.

       The dispositions for SSIIGGKKIILLLL and SSIIGGSSTTOOPP cannot be changed.

       The ssiigghhoolldd() function adds _s_i_g to the calling process’s signal mask.

       The  ssiiggrreellssee()  function removes _s_i_g from the calling process’s signal
       mask.

       The ssiiggiiggnnoorree() function sets the disposition of _s_i_g to SSIIGG__IIGGNN.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       On success, ssiiggsseett() returns SSIIGG__HHOOLLDD if _s_i_g  was  blocked  before  the
       call, or the signal’s previous disposition if it was not blocked before
       the  call.   On  error, ssiiggsseett() returns -1, with _e_r_r_n_o set to indicate
       the error.  (But see BUGS below.)

       The ssiigghhoolldd(), ssiiggrreellssee(), and ssiiggiiggnnoorree() functions return 0  on  suc‐
       cess; on error, these functions return -1 and set _e_r_r_n_o to indicate the
       error.

