NNOOTTEESS
       The ssiiggsseett() function provides reliable signal handling  semantics  (as
       when calling ssiiggaaccttiioonn(2) with _s_a___m_a_s_k equal to 0).

       On  System  V,  the ssiiggnnaall() function provides unreliable semantics (as
       when  calling  ssiiggaaccttiioonn(2)  with  _s_a___m_a_s_k  equal  to  _S_A___R_E_S_E_T_H_A_N_D   _|
       _S_A___N_O_D_E_F_E_R).    On   BSD,   ssiiggnnaall()   provides   reliable   semantics.
       POSIX.1‐2001  leaves  these  aspects  of  ssiiggnnaall()  unspecified.    See
       ssiiggnnaall(2) for further details.

       In  order  to  wait  for  a  signal,  BSD  and System V both provided a
       function named ssiiggppaauussee(3), but this function has a different  argument
       on the two systems.  See ssiiggppaauussee(3) for details.

BBUUGGSS
       Before glibc 2.2, ssiiggsseett() did not unblock _s_i_g if _d_i_s_p was specified as
       a value other than SSIIGG__HHOOLLDD.

       Before  glibc  2.5,  ssiiggsseett()  does  not  correctly return the previous
       disposition of the signal in two cases.  First, if _d_i_s_p is specified as
       SSIIGG__HHOOLLDD, then a successful ssiiggsseett() always returns SSIIGG__HHOOLLDD.  Instead,
       it should return the previous disposition of  the  signal  (unless  the
       signal  was  blocked,  in  which  case  SSIIGG__HHOOLLDD  should  be returned).
       Second, if the signal is currently blocked, then the return value of  a
       successful   ssiiggsseett()   should  be  SSIIGG__HHOOLLDD.   Instead,  the  previous
       disposition of the signal is returned.  These problems have been  fixed
       since glibc 2.5.

